Spring Framework vs Spring Boot
-------------------------------
1. Purpose
    Spring Framework: A comprehensive framework for enterprise-level Java development. It provides flexibility in building Java applications by using dependency injection (DI), aspect-oriented programming (AOP), and a wide range of tools.
    Spring Boot: A framework built on top of Spring Framework. It simplifies the development of Spring applications by providing pre-configured setups, reducing boilerplate code, and offering an opinionated approach.
2. Configuration
    Spring Framework:
    Requires significant manual configuration (XML or annotations).
    Developers need to define beans, dependencies, and other configurations explicitly.
    Spring Boot:
    Offers auto-configuration.
    Reduces configuration effort with defaults based on conventions.
    Uses application.properties or application.yml for external configurations.
3. Startup Time
    Spring Framework:
    May have a slower startup due to manual configurations and bean initialization.
    Spring Boot:
    Faster startup due to auto-configuration and embedded servers like Tomcat or Jetty.
    4. Dependency Management
    Spring Framework:
    Dependencies must be managed manually. Developers choose and configure all required dependencies.
    Spring Boot:
    Comes with a pre-defined spring-boot-starter dependency model, which bundles commonly used dependencies.
5. Embedded Servers
    Spring Framework:
    No built-in support for embedded servers; requires external configuration for deployment on servers like Tomcat or WebLogic.
    Spring Boot:
    Comes with embedded servers (Tomcat, Jetty, or Undertow) for running standalone applications.
6. Development Speed
    Spring Framework:
    More flexible but slower to set up and develop due to extensive configuration.
    Spring Boot:
    Faster development cycle with defaults and built-in tools like Spring Initializr.
7. Microservices
    Spring Framework:
    Can be used for microservices but requires more effort to set up compared to Spring Boot.
    Spring Boot:
    Designed for microservices architecture, with features like REST support, embedded servers, and Spring Cloud integration.

